ary_7", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level."}, {"type": "secondary_8", "content": "Discusses a range of options for potential actions at work through the correct use of grammar, vocabulary; level of formality and purpose, and cohesion and coherence aspects appropriate to this level."}], "type": "objectives"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Present Perfect Continuous vs. Present Perfect Tenses"}, {"type": "describer", "content": "You use these tenses  to express that an action began in the past and is still going on or has just finished. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "We use the <span class=\"italic\">Present Perfect tense</span> mainly to express that an action is completed or to emphasize the result. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "Forms:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Regular verbs: form of <span class=\"italic\">have</span> + base form of verb + <span class=\"italic\">-ed</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">Example: I have worked at the bakery for six years.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<br/>Irregular verbs: form of <span class=\"italic\">have</span> + past participle of irregular verbs"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">Example: I have written a number of books in the last two years.</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<br/>We use the <span class=\"italic\">Present Perfect Continuous tense</span> to emphasize the duration or continuous course of an action."}, {"type": "text", "content": "Form:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Form of <span class=\"italic\">have</span> + <span class=\"italic\">been</span> + base form of verb + <span class=\"italic\">-ing</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<span class=\"italic\">Example: We have been walking for two hours!</span>"}, {"type": "text", "content": "Because you are talking about the continuous course of an action, you often use a time marker with Present Perfect Continuous tense, like:"}, {"type": "text", "content": "<ul><li>for two hours</li><li>for a long time</li><li>for ages</li></ul>"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've been in touch with the editors of Cambridge Life. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've decided it would be boring to be a scientist. "}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've been wanting to be a journalist for ages!"}, {"type": "example", "content": "I've been planning to enrol in some courses for a while."}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "heading", "content": "Discussing How to Deal with Problems "}, {"type": "describer", "content": "When you want to offer advice or give solutions to a problem, you use modals like <span class=\"italic\">might, should</span> or <span class=\"italic\">could</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "These modals can go at the beginning of the sentence, or after a clause starting with <span class=\"italic\">\u201cIf ... \u201d</span>."}, {"type": "text", "content": "The modal is followed by a verb in the base form. You do not use \u201cto\u201d. "}, {"type": "text", "content": "I would/I'd ... <br/>I wouldn't ... <br/>You could ... <br/>You should ..."}, {"type": "example", "content": "I would keep my options open."}, {"type": "example", "content": "You could talk to a few more people first."}, {"type": "example", "content": "Maybe you should think a bit more about this idea. "}], "type": "learning_points"}, {"contents": [{"type": "name", "content": "it looks promising"}, {"type": "definition", "content": "expression meaning \u201cI feel positive or optimistic\u201d about something"}, {"type": "part_speech", "content": "(expression)"}, {"type": "example", "content": "Example: Well, it's not 100% sure yet, but it looks promising! "}, {"type": "audio", "content": "engenn7le15ob10aud01"}], "type": "target_word"}, {"contents": [{"type": "text", "content